74:All participants were part of the EU-AIMS/AIMS-2-TRIALS Longitudinal European Autism Project (LEAP) cohort. Access to the data is currently granted to consortium members. Full details of the LEAP study design can be found in 1,2 .  The LEAP protocols are available at https://www.eu-aims.eu/fileadmin/websites/eu-aims/media/EU-AIMS\_LEAP/EU-AIMS-
78:from  all  participants  or  their  legal  guardians  (for  participants  &lt;18  years).  Participants  did receive  compensation  for  the  study  visits.  All  autistic  participants  had  an  existing  clinical diagnosis of autism according to DSM-IV 3 , DSM-IV-TR 4 , DSM-5 5 or ICD-10 6 criteria. In the autism  group,  diagnosis  was  confirmed  using  the  combined  information  of  gold-standard diagnostic instruments, the Autism Diagnostic Interview-Revised 7 (ADI-R) and the Autism Diagnostic  Observation  Schedule  (ADOS) 8 .  Appropriate  to  a  multi-centre  study,  quality control procedures were in place around training, and data collection/entry. Cross-site training sessions for collecting clinical data were put in place, the ADOS and ADI-R were administered and scored by qualified/certified personnel and the study was regularly monitored according to Good  Clinical  Practice  (GCP)  standards.  Participants  underwent  comprehensive  clinical, cognitive and MRI assessment at one of six collaborating sites: the Institute of Psychiatry, Psychology and Neurosciences, King's College London (KCL), London, United Kingdom; Autism  Research  Centre  at  the  University  of  Cambridge,  Cambridge,  United  Kingdom; Radboud  University  Nijmegen  Medical  Centre,  Nijmegen,  the  Netherlands;  University Medical  Centre  Utrecht,  Utrecht,  the  Netherlands;  Central  Institute  of  Mental  Health, Mannheim, Germany; and University Campus Bio-Medico, Rome, Italy. Exclusion criteria included the presence of any MRI  contraindications (e.g., metal implants, braces, claustrophobia)  or  failure  to  give  informed  written  consent  to  MRI  scanning,  as  well  as significant hearing or visual impairments not corrected by glasses or hearing aids.  Individuals with an IQ below 50 were excluded, as core measures (e.g., most cognitive tasks and MRI scanning without sedation) were deemed difficult to administer in this group. In the autistic group, psychiatric conditions (except for psychosis or bipolar disorder) were allowed as up to 70% of autistic people have one or more psychiatric disorders 9 and reflect DSM-5 that allows co-occurring psychiatric disorders alongside an autism diagnosis. In the non-autistic individuals, on the other hand, parent- or self-report of a psychiatric disorder or a T-score of 70 or higher on the self-report or parent-report form of the Social Responsiveness Scale-2 were exclusion criteria. A history of alcohol and/or substance abuse or dependence in the past year was an exclusion criterion for both groups. Furthermore, individuals were included if they were on stable medication (at least 8 weeks) at entrance point and over the course of the baseline visit. This is supported by the fact that a significant percentage of autistic children and adults in Europe (30-50%) 10 and even higher percentages in the USA (up to 70%) are prescribed at least  one  medication  to  address  symptoms  like  aggression,  anxiety,  hyperactivity,  or  sleep disturbances 11 .  Information on concurrent medication use was collected at the institute visit and substances were mapped to the Anatomical Therapeutic Chemical (ATC) classification
82:Based  on  parent-  or  self-reported  ethnicity,  most  participants  included  in  this  study  were Caucasian  white  (74.5%,  N=152).  The  remaining  participants  were  described  as  either  of mixed  race  (6.4%,  N=13),  or  Asian  (2%,  N=4).  For  17.1%  of  participants  information  on ethnicity was either not provided (14.7%, N=30) or missing (2.4%, N=5) (see Supplementary Table 10).
86:- ∞ Structural  T1-weighted  images with  excessive  head  motion  (N=37),  clinically (mostly)  non-significant  atypicalities  (N=21)  and  failed  preprocessing  (N=3)  were excluded. A full quality control report was generated by the CAT SPM pipeline for each participant. This included quantitative quality measures summarized in weighted overall image quality ratings ranging from 0.5 (best) to 10.5 (worst). Autistic and nonautistic individuals did not differ on measures of image quality derived from SPM after exclusions ( W =4926, p =0.52).
93:Applying all exclusion criteria resulted in a sample of 204 individuals (99 autism, 105 nonautistic individuals [NAI]) between 7 and 30 years of age matched for age, full-scale IQ (FIQ), verbal IQ (VIQ), performance IQ (PIQ), structural image quality, EEG trial length and head motion in rs-fMRI and task-fMRI across diagnostic groups. For details see Table 1.
99:Sex (biological attribute) of the participants was determined based on self- or parent-report. No information on the gender (shaped by social and cultural circumstances) of participants was collected.
